# Graycell Website --- AI Coding Rules

## 1. Project identity

Project: Graycell Group corporate engineering website.

Primary brand: **Graycell**

Working positioning: **Engineered Power. Built to Perform.**

The site represents Graycell as a master brand / group presentation. The
supplied catalogues reference: - Graycell Power Solutions Pvt. Ltd. -
Graycell Energy LLP

These legal/company names are content data, not permission to invent
ownership, capabilities, certifications, or corporate relationships.

Primary public enquiry email: - `sales@graycellpower.com`

Do not publish director names or director phone numbers.

## 2. Core product/source truth

The supplied Graycell catalogues are the primary source for current
product/content claims.

Source-supported areas include: - Oil-filled distribution transformers -
Dry-type distribution transformers - Aluminium foil-wound transformers -
Copper-wound / copper-related transformer offerings - Natural ester oil
transformers - Hermetically sealed and non-sealed transformers - Smart
monitoring / digital diagnostics references - Compact substations - MV
switchgear / VCB references - Related MV electrical-system offerings -
Manufacturing, winding, testing and quality-process claims - Industrial,
commercial, renewable-energy and utility applications - 25 kVA--2500 kVA
and up-to-33 kV references in the supplied material

Treat every technical range, standard, approval, partner relationship,
certification, project, client logo and service claim as
**source-controlled content**. If a claim is not supported by approved
project data or a current company source, do not invent it.

## 3. Technology stack

Preferred frontend: - Next.js with App Router - TypeScript - Tailwind
CSS - React - Framer Motion for UI motion - GSAP only for complex
scroll/engineering sequences where justified - CSS for simple
transitions - Lucide or a controlled SVG icon set

The architecture must be CMS/API-ready even though Version 1 uses local
mock data.

## 4. Architecture rules

Use: - reusable components - typed domain models - configuration-driven
content - route-level page templates - separation of content/data from
presentation - service/repository abstraction for future API integration

Do not: - hardcode product copy into multiple components - hardcode
contact details in multiple files - create a separate component for
every product - duplicate product page layouts - couple UI components
directly to future backend implementation - expose internal/confidential
engineering data

All contact information must originate from a single site configuration
object.

## 5. Data rules

Every domain object must have: - stable `id` - human-readable `slug` -
`status` - timestamps where appropriate - explicit relationships -
optional media/document references

Use TypeScript types/interfaces for all data.

Never use `any` for domain data.

Use `unknown` at external boundaries and validate before use.

## 6. Product rules

Product pages must be generated from structured product data.

At minimum support: - product identity - category - summary - capacity -
voltage - applications - technologies - specifications - features -
documents - images - related products - enquiry configuration

A product may be marked: - `manufactured` - `partner` - `associated` -
`solution` - `draft`

Do not imply that a partner/associated product is manufactured by
Graycell unless explicitly confirmed.

## 7. Design rules

The visual language is: - German-inspired industrial precision - premium
power engineering - restrained - technical - grid-based -
high-contrast - data-led

Do not use: - excessive glassmorphism - oversized rounded cards - random
gradients - generic SaaS visuals - decorative lightning graphics - fake
German flags/colors - fake factory scenes - fake engineers - fabricated
projects

Real Graycell assets take priority over AI-generated imagery.

AI-generated imagery may be used for conceptual/atmospheric visuals, but
must never be presented as a real Graycell factory, employee,
certification, product, project or installation.

## 8. Motion rules

Motion must explain: - electrical flow - engineering process -
assembly - product relationships - data - navigation

Prefer subtle, controlled movement.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Do not animate every component.

## 9. Forms

The enquiry/RFQ form must be production-minded.

Minimum fields: - name - company - email - phone - requirement type -
application - capacity - primary voltage - secondary voltage -
quantity - location - special requirements - specification upload -
message

Frontend validation must be implemented.

Do not expose secrets or email-service credentials in client code.

## 10. WhatsApp

The WhatsApp number is configuration data.

Do not invent the number.

Use an empty/null configuration until the approved number is supplied.

## 11. Security

-   Never commit secrets.
-   Never put API keys in client-side source.
-   Validate uploaded files.
-   Restrict upload size/type.
-   Sanitize rendered rich text.
-   Validate all API responses.
-   Use server-side handling for email/API credentials.
-   Do not expose internal documents.
-   Do not expose unpublished CMS data.

## 12. Accessibility

Required: - semantic HTML - keyboard navigation - visible focus states -
proper form labels - alt text - sufficient contrast - reduced-motion
support - logical heading hierarchy

## 13. Performance

Prioritize: - optimized images - AVIF/WebP - lazy loading - responsive
image sizes - code splitting - dynamic imports for heavy visual
modules - compressed video - minimal client-side JavaScript

A premium site must remain fast.

## 14. SEO

Every page must have: - unique title - meta description - canonical
URL - semantic headings - meaningful body content - Open Graph
metadata - appropriate JSON-LD where applicable

Never use keyword stuffing.

See `ai-seo-spec.md`.

## 15. AI behaviour

Before creating a new component: 1. Search existing component hierarchy.
2. Reuse an existing component if possible. 3. Extend an existing
component if the behaviour belongs to an existing abstraction. 4. Create
a new component only when it represents a genuinely reusable concept.

Before adding a claim: 1. Check source data. 2. Check approved content.
3. If unsupported, use a placeholder marked
`CONTENT_REQUIRES_CONFIRMATION`. 4. Never silently manufacture a claim.

Before adding a new product: 1. Add it to the typed data model. 2. Add
required metadata. 3. Add related technologies/applications. 4. Add
approved media/documents. 5. Verify its ownership/manufacturing status.

## 16. Definition of done

A feature is not complete until: - TypeScript passes - lint passes -
responsive states work - keyboard interaction works - reduced motion is
respected - loading/error/empty states exist where relevant - SEO
metadata exists - no hardcoded contact values exist outside
configuration - no unsupported technical claims are introduced - no
duplicated UI pattern has been created unnecessarily
